Invented by Johannes Gutenberg around 1440 in Mainz, Germany, the movable type printing press revolutionized the spread of information and permanently altered the course of human history. By combining existing technologies like the screw press and oil-based ink with the innovative concept of reusable metal type, Gutenberg made book production significantly faster and more affordable. This breakthrough not only accelerated the dissemination of knowledge but also fueled the Protestant Reformation, the Renaissance, and the Scientific Revolution
